Ladybird lays out the text nodes and child elements of an SVG text
content element in document order, as one stream of characters. It
collapses white space across element boundaries, leaving the surviving
space in the text node its run starts in. And it paints text that
follows a child element after that child. Blink does the same:
SvgTextLayoutAlgorithm::Layout() positions one stream of characters,
InlineItemsBuilder’s AppendCollapseWhitespace() collapses white space
across inline boundaries, and the text fragments paint in inline order.
So does WebKit, in its SVGTextLayoutAlgorithm and its inline content
painter. And so does Gecko, in SVGTextFrame::ResolvePositionsForNode()
and DoGlyphPositioning(), with SVGTextFrame::PaintSVG() painting
the rendered runs in order.

In Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5, the two new
tests and the cases added to the link test give the same character
positions, with two exceptions. Firefox applies the x attribute of a
<tspan> with display:none to the text that follows it, where Chrome,
WebKit, and Ladybird don’t. And where a <tspan> and its parent have
different white-space values, WebKit drops a space that Chrome,
Firefox, and Ladybird keep. The bounding boxes the tests print are
Chrome’s: Firefox’s are 2px wider, and WebKit reports the box of the
whole <text> for a <tspan>.

All three engines skip the x, y, dx, and dy of an element without
characters, and none of them renders a <text> nested in a <text>.

Hit testing differs from Blink where an element’s own text overlaps
one of its child elements: Ladybird still tests all of the element’s
own text after its children.
